storage/sync
- read-only organisations (built; see docs/SYNC_ARCHITECTURE.md): the Drive-capabilities trigger can't fire under drive.file (tested); the role will come from the Worker broker, see the invite item below
- variants and editing a chart: plan in docs/VARIANTS.md (origin on each variant, the organisation's default, a variant step in the song picker, a versions list, a chart editor for fixes and arrangements)
- background syncing (periodic, and fallbacks)
- conflict resolution (currently Drive wins when edited on two devices, and the local edit is
  silently dropped). Idea: keep the losing edit as a conflict copy of the setlist instead.
  Left for now: with immediate sync of the changed record (below) two devices rarely edit the
  same setlist from the same base, so it matters much less. The random sync tests (`conflicts`
  mode, tests/drive-sync-random.test.ts) already check convergence, a safety net for this change
- several people editing a setlist (designed 2026-10-16 in docs/SYNC_ARCHITECTURE.md, "Several
  people editing a setlist"; not built). Decided: start by blocking the sync of writes from
  non-owners (only the owner's edits to a setlist are uploaded); their edits stay on their device
  in a layer over the shared setlist, which needs building first (same layer as the read-only
  item under "editing"). Later: an editors list, then suggestions to the owner (accept or
  reject). Decided: createdBy and ownerId (when different) both have edit rights; an organisation admin
  role later. Open: open to all in the organisation by default, or only those. The layer must
  show, simply: one indicator that something differs from the official version, and a way to view
  what (see the doc). The Worker broker later enforces it and detects conflicts
- delete setlists (owner only, trashed in Drive on sync, removed on other devices):
  designed in docs/SYNC_ARCHITECTURE.md, not built
- immediate background sync of just the changed record (setlist/song), in the
  service worker, with full sync as the backstop: designed in docs/SYNC_ARCHITECTURE.md,
  not built. Later: durable offline write queue.
- richer PWA install UI (Chrome's devtools manifest tab warns): add "screenshots" to
  public/manifest.webmanifest, at least one with form_factor "wide" (desktop) and one without it
  or "narrow" (mobile). Generate them with Playwright from seeded data, using a public-domain
  hymn (no copyrighted lyrics), and keep them out of the precache if they are large
- privacy-preserving analytics (cookieless, like Plausible; GoatCounter, Umami and Cloudflare Web
  Analytics are the alternatives). Proxy the script and its event endpoint through the worker so
  the CSP stays 'self' and ad blockers don't drop it. Constraints: events are lost while the
  device is offline (the iPad often is), so counts undercount; never send song titles, lyrics,
  setlist names or the ids in /setlist/<id> and /songs/<id> (count the page type only); never send
  Google sign-in data (email, name, id): the OAuth verification is for Limited Use; no events in
  dev or the Playwright tests; update public/privacy.html (it says no analytics) in the same
  change.
  Song popularity: one event when a song is added to a setlist, with its CCLI song number as a
  property (a public catalogue id, not user data), only when it is all digits (4 to 9 long), so
  free text can never leak. Never fall back to the title (it may be a private song). Songs with no
  CCLI number are not counted. The chart's number is in song.metadata.ccliSongNumber or .ccli (getSongWithContent). Plausible custom props: check which plan; GoatCounter can use an event
  name like song-added/<number>; Cloudflare Web Analytics has no events. Say it in the policy too
- proofread the sample charts (public/samples): the words were written from memory and the chords
  and the time and tempo are my guesses. Check the words against a source before real use
- check on a real iPhone/iPad (Safari): the first visit with no reload (public/boot.js waits two
  seconds for the worker to claim the page, then reloads), and the card's install instructions
- first-load speed: a cold first visit's LCP waits on one more request wave (the home view's
  chunks load after the entry runs). If it feels slow on a real device, preload the landing view
  with the entry (about 15 KB gzipped more for every route). Measure on the live site first
- Lighthouse leftovers: pinch zoom is off on purpose (accessibility flags it); the llms.txt and
  ai-catalog audits are ignored
- the Content-Security-Policy is enforced (public/_headers). Not yet checked: the service worker's
