 - - we want to be able to forward an email sent by others and import a setlist
  from it. Expect attached PDFs, download and extract ccli numbers and match
  against song db.  If the song is missing, ideally create a shell that can be
  imported over from songselect. Ideally, we can sue "Share To" type
  interaction to share the email and attachements locally, but maybe we need
  some kind of email address to process this as an anonymous share and reply with the link to you
  Built so far: the parser (src/js/email-setlist.ts, tests/email-setlist.node.test.ts), pure text in,
  setlist out. Decisions: the leader's name and email are kept as owner/ownerId whether or not they
  are in any organisation; songs get their order and keys from the list in the body (numbered, or
  OnSong's bullets), else the attachments' order (orderFromList false: say it is a guess); the same
  song in two keys is one song, first key, with a note "May also be in E"; a missing date is asked
  for, defaulting to nearestSunday(); a date whose weekday is wrong ("Sunday 8th Nov" on a
  Saturday) becomes the nearest right weekday and must be confirmed (dateWarning); the subject is
  the setlist's name ("Prayer day" is one), and a date in it is not removed.
  Built: the /import-setlist review page (src/components/setlist-email-import.ts),
  matching by CCLI number, then title, then close titles to confirm, then, if no title is close, a
  line of the songs' words (src/js/import-plan.ts: leaders call a song by its chorus, e.g. "Your Grace
  Is Enough" for "This Is Our God"; never picked for the person).
  Built too: the email Worker (worker/src/email.ts; docs/EMAIL_IMPORT.md): a forwarded email gets a
  reply with /import-setlist#<setlist> (src/js/email-link.ts), tried in workerd with wrangler dev.
  Set up by hand in the Cloudflare dashboard (Email Routing on chordless.app, an address that
  sends to the Worker); tried with real forwards.
  Built too: matching songs of the other databases (copied into the library when the setlist is
  made); updating the setlist that has the date (the same leader's is the default, the changes
  are shown first); rate limits on the Worker (per sender and overall); createdBy on setlists
  (recorded, nothing decides by it yet); no .eml file picker (only the Worker reads emails;
  the app reads a PDF only when one is shared to it, see docs/EMAIL_IMPORT.md). WhatsApp can
  share neither text messages nor a selection of them, so a setlist cannot come from it: one
  downloaded PDF at a time can. Other ways in are not built: Share To (email apps
  do not share an email to a web app) and a pasted link are not worth it, as the reply's link
  opens the installed app.
  Still to build: use of createdBy (an imported setlist's owner is the leader, so the importer
  could not delete it under the owner rule: allow the owner or the creator;
  docs/SYNC_ARCHITECTURE.md).
- email import, a rule: the PDFs are never imported as charts. They are read only for their title,
  key, tempo and CCLI number (src/js/pdf-text.ts). A song that is not in the library (by CCLI
  number, then by title) has to be imported from SongSelect first, with the bookmarklet: the review
  page (/import-setlist) links to its SongSelect page (songSelectLink in
  src/js/songselect-link.ts: /songs/<ccli>, else a search for the title; the search URL could not
  be checked from here, SongSelect refuses automated requests, so check it by hand), looks again
  when the person comes back, and won't make the setlist until each song is in the library or left
  out. No placeholder songs: they would sit in the shared library with no chart, and a title
  that differs from SongSelect's would never be replaced. The Worker keeps no PDF either: it
  replies with a link holding only the metadata.
- email import, from surveying 61 real emails (tests/emails-private.node.test.ts): about half the
  charts have no CCLI number. They are old OnSong PDFs from before SongSelect, which people still
  use: a different version of the song, often under another title. So a chart with no CCLI number
  must not be taken as the song: match the title against the library first (and the other
  databases), and offer replacing it with the SongSelect version, which is how the library stays
  one shared set. Leaders also write the key with no "in" ("Behold him Bb"), number the PDFs'
  filenames to give the order, and put a note or a second title in brackets.
- email import, later: mass-import the emails that matched no setlist as new historic setlists, once the import works
  properly: the leader, date and songs from importFromEmail, songs matched to the library by
  CCLI number then title, with shells and SongSelect links for the rest. Many have no date (events
  like "Prayer Day"), so it must ask.
- notes on a setlist song (SetlistEntry.notes) are not shown anywhere yet: show them in live mode
  and the overview, and let them be edited. The email import fills them in ("fairly empty to
  start", "May also be in E").

